  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 315 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Breezy, sunny, and dry fall weather continues \nthis week.\n\nAn expansive area of high pressure stretches from the Gulf Coast \nnorthward into southern Ontario and Quebec this afternoon. The pesky \nlow pressure system remains offshore (stacked under an upper low), \nthough visible satellite imagery shows all the clouds associated \nwith this system offshore of the region. Overall, a beautiful day \nacross our CWA this afternoon with temperatures in the upper 60s to \nlower 70s and dew points in the 30s and 40s. Breezy conditions \ncontinue along the coast where the pressure gradient remains \ncompressed between the high and low pressure systems. Another chilly \nnight with lows by Wednesday morning in the low to mid 40s, except \nstaying in the 50s along the coast where the northerly breeze off \nthe water will moderate the temperatures. \n\nSimilar synoptic pattern and resultant wx continues for the \nremainder of the week. Most model guidance suggests the low will \nprobably retrograde back toward the coast by ~50 miles tonight into \nWednesday. Therefore, breezy conditions are likely to continue at \nthe coast. There could also be some increase in stratocumulus for \nthe eastern ~third of the area for most of the day tomorrow. \nTemperatures will generally be a few degrees cooler than today and \nrange from the lower 60s at the coast to the mid to potentially \nupper 60s inland. The stacked low offshore finally departs to the \nnortheast at a brisk speed by Wednesday night. This is as it gets a \nkick from a digging, northern stream trough over the northeast CONUS \nand SE Canada. High temps Thursday into the weekend will be in the \n60s for most, with overnight lows in the upper 30s to mid 40s inland \nand upper 40s to lower 50s at the coast. The dry wx persists through \nthe weekend and likely into next week as well. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Prolonged onshore flow will lead to multiple \nelevated tide cycles this week resulting in rounds of coastal \nflooding.\n\nGenerally 1-2 foot anomalies across the lower bay and ocean this \nafternoon and slowly rising as water levels build due to the \npersistent north to northeast wind leading to some nuisance to minor \ntidal flooding across the advisory area today. Due to the \nastronomical tides being lower with tonight's high tide cycle, am \nexpecting similar flooding with the overnight high tide as the \nanomalies are forecast to be a little higher than today.\n\nThe ETSS, PETSS and STOFS continue to suggest the anomalies will \ncontinue to increase on Wednesday with 2-3 ft anomalies possible. \nThis makes sense given that the coastal low may back westward \nslightly tonight leading to a slight increase of wind speed. This \nmay allow some of the sites more prone to flooding (Lynnhaven, \nSmithfield, Bayford, etc.) to touch moderate flooding during the \nhigh tides tomorrow afternoon and perhaps Wed night. However, will \nnot issue any watch or warning at this time as the models have \ntrended too high for this entire event so far and the confidence is \njust not there to issue any watch or warning at this time. \n\nThe low finally starts to move northeast by Thursday, which should \nallow for the winds to subside and also the water levels, except for \nthe upper bay where water levels may actually rise some as some \nwater gets trapped in the bay in response to stronger flood tides at \nthe mouth of the bay owing to the weaker winds.",
